<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/intermediate/sources-of-law">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    "The law" is not one book — it is a <strong>hierarchy of sources with a strict chain of
    command</strong>: constitutions above, then statutes, then regulations, then judicial
    precedent, then custom. Every legal argument begins by locating its authority in this
    hierarchy, because the resolution of any conflict depends on which source outranks which.
    This page works through the hierarchy from the top down, and then does the thing lawyers
    actually spend their lives doing: reading the texts — because a source only matters through
    its interpretation.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four rungs and the supremacy rule that orders them" widget="equationStep" config={sourcesCfg} />

  <h2>Constitutions: written and unwritten frames</h2>
  <p>
    A constitution is the supreme law that creates the other lawmakers and limits them. Most
    states have a codified one — a single authoritative document, entrenched so that it can
    only be amended by a special procedure (in the United States, approval by three quarters
    of the states, achieved only 27 times since 1787). The courts then police the hierarchy:
    in <em>Marbury v Madison</em> (1803) the US Supreme Court claimed and established the power
    of <strong>judicial review</strong> — striking down statutes that conflict with the
    constitution.
  </p>
  <p>
    The United Kingdom is the great exception: its constitution is <strong>uncodified</strong> —
    no single document, but a working body of statute law (Magna Carta 1215, the Bill of
    Rights 1689, the Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949, the Human Rights Act 1998), common law
    principles, and <em>conventions</em> (binding political customs, like the royal assent
    convention, that no court enforces but everyone obeys). The supreme principle is
    <strong>parliamentary sovereignty</strong>: Parliament can make or unmake any law, and no
    court can strike an Act down. That flips the American picture — judicial review in the UK
    tests <em>ministers and regulations</em> against statutes, not statutes against a
    constitution. The Human Rights Act 1998 is the nearest thing to a constitutional statute
    (courts cannot strike laws down but can issue "declarations of incompatibility"), and
    devolution to Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland since 1998 has added entrenched-ish
    statutes to a system whose genius — and fragility — is that Parliament itself is the
    ceiling.
  </p>
  <p>
    That fragility is why the <strong>rule of law</strong> sits next to the constitution as a
    founding idea. A.V. Dicey's classic formulation (1885) has three parts: no one may be
    punished except for a breach of law established in the ordinary courts (no arbitrary
    power); everyone, however powerful, is subject to the same law; and the constitution is
    the <em>result</em> of ordinary law, not a separate source. The rule of law is not a legal
    source that outranks Parliament — that would contradict sovereignty — but a political
    morality the system tries to honour through its procedures: independent judges, public
    hearings, reasoned decisions. When lawyers say a proposal "threatens the rule of law",
    they mean it breaks those habits, not that a court can void it.
  </p>

  <h2>Statutes: how Parliament makes law</h2>
  <p>
    Statutes (Acts of Parliament) are the main source of <em>new</em> law, and the process is
    deliberately obstacle-rich. A Bill passes <strong>first reading</strong> (formal
    introduction), <strong>second reading</strong> (the principle debate), <strong>committee
    stage</strong> (line-by-line scrutiny, where most amendments happen), <strong>report</strong>
    and <strong>third reading</strong> in the Commons; then the same gauntlet in the House of
    Lords; finally <strong>Royal Assent</strong> (by convention, never refused). The Lords can
    delay money bills by a month and other bills by up to a year; the Parliament Acts let the
    Commons override them entirely — used only a handful of times (the War Crimes Act 1991,
    the Hunting Act 2004). In a typical year Parliament passes a few dozen public general
    Acts. Each Act is drafted with almost mathematical care, which is exactly why so much
    litigation is about what its words <em>reach</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Delegated legislation: the detail layer</h2>
  <p>
    Parliament cannot write every rule: modern government needs thousands of technical rules —
    pension uprating, medication licences, speed limits — updated constantly, by people who
    understand them. So an Act <em>enables</em> a minister (or local authority, or the Crown)
    to make <strong>delegated legislation</strong>: statutory instruments (around 3,000 a year,
    outnumbering Acts nearly a hundred to one), byelaws, and orders in council. This is the
    efficiency-versus-scrutiny trade-off in law's purest form, and both safeguards exist
    because of it. <strong>Parliamentary</strong> safeguards: some instruments need active
    approval (affirmative resolution), most stand unless objected to (negative resolution),
    and a joint committee reviews them technically. <strong>Judicial</strong> safeguards: an
    instrument beyond the power the parent Act granted is <strong>ultra vires</strong> — void.
    The doctrine bites hard in emergencies: in 2021 the courts ruled government pandemic
    procurement guidance beyond the Public Health Act powers it claimed. The lesson runs
    through the whole topic: delegation multiplies law-making power, so validity always
    traces back to the authority that was granted.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> delegated legislation is not "weaker law" or
    "unofficial". A statutory instrument binds exactly as an Act does in practice — it is
    simply vulnerable to being voided for going beyond its enabling power.
  </p>

  <h2>Statutory interpretation: the reading rules</h2>
  <p>
    Words meet the world messily, so courts use four named approaches. The
    <strong>literal (plain meaning) rule</strong> applies the ordinary words even to absurd
    results: in <em>Fisher v Bell</em> (1961) a flick knife displayed in a shop window was not
    "offering it for sale" because contract law treats displays as invitations to treat —
    so the prosecution failed and Parliament promptly fixed the gap. In <em>Whiteley v
    Chappell</em> (1908) impersonating a <em>dead</em> voter escaped the offence of
    impersonating "any person entitled to vote". The <strong>golden rule</strong> modifies
    literalism to avoid absurdity: in <em>Adler v George</em> (1964) a defendant already
    <em>inside</em> a prohibited RAF station was convicted under a provision about being "in
    the vicinity of" it. The <strong>mischief rule</strong> (from <em>Heydon's Case</em>, 1584)
    asks what problem the Act was passed to cure: <em>Smith v Hughes</em> (1960) held that
    prostitutes soliciting from balconies and windows were "in a street" within the Street
    Offences Act 1959 — the mischief was public solicitation, not the pavement. The modern
    <strong>purposive approach</strong> asks what the provision was <em>for</em>, using
    purpose over typography: <em>Royal College of Nursing v DHSS</em> (1981) read "registered
    medical practitioner" in the Abortion Act 1967 to cover newly trained nurses supervising
    early abortions, because medicine had moved on since 1967.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> these rules are not a code — the same case can be argued
    under two approaches, and the choice itself is the argument. Critics of literalism say it
    lets drafters' accidents defeat Parliament's purpose; critics of purposivism say it lets
    judges rewrite statutes. Both complaints are true, which is why the topic exists.
  </p>

  <h2>Judicial precedent: judge-made law</h2>
  <p>
    The common law method is <strong>stare decisis</strong> — "stand by things decided": like
    cases decided alike, which delivers predictability, equality and efficiency in one rule.
    Only one part of a judgment binds: the <strong>ratio decidendi</strong> — the legal rule
    required to decide the case on its material facts. Everything else — hypotheticals,
    commentary, extra reasons — is <strong>obiter dicta</strong>, persuasive but not
    binding. Binding is hierarchical: the UK Supreme Court binds every court below it; the
    Court of Appeal normally binds itself, with escape hatches (<em>Young v Bristol Aeroplane
    Co</em>, 1944) where decisions conflict or were given per incuriam; and since the Practice
    Statement of 1966 the top court may depart from its own past decisions "when it appears
    right to do so". Judges manage precedent three ways: <strong>following</strong> the ratio,
    <strong>distinguishing</strong> a case on a material difference of facts, or
    <strong>overruling</strong> it outright.
  </p>
  <p>
    The method's power shows in its landmarks. <em>Donoghue v Stevenson</em> (1932) — the
    snail in the ginger-beer bottle, where no contract existed — generated the
    <strong>neighbour principle</strong> and with it the modern tort of negligence, applied
    case by case ever since. <em>R v R</em> (1991) held that a husband could rape his wife
    because "the fiction" of marital consent had become an anachronism — judge-made change
    that Parliament then confirmed by statute. That pairing is the honest summary: precedent
    builds the law incrementally, and statute ratifies or redirects it.
  </p>
  <p>
    How do precedent and statute interact when they collide? Statute wins: Parliament can
    override any common-law rule by clear words, as it did after <em>R v R</em> and, in the
    classic demonstration, when the War Damage Act 1965 was passed to reverse <em>Burmah Oil
    v Lord Advocate</em> (1965) — a compensation ruling about wartime destruction that
    Parliament disliked. But judicial precedent also
    governs the statutes themselves, because every Act passes through the interpretation
    rules of the previous section before it applies to a case. The two sources are therefore
    not parallel but braided: precedent makes the law that statutes revise, and precedent
    also supplies the reading rules that decide what the revisions mean. A working lawyer
    rarely asks "which source applies?" — the answer is usually both at once, one constraining
    the other.
  </p>

  <h2>Law reform and the European layer</h2>
  <p>
    Sources need maintenance. The <strong>Law Commission</strong> (established 1965) is the
    standing law-reform body: it reviews whole areas of law, consults, and drafts
    consolidation bills — dozens of its reports have become Acts. Reform also comes through
    Royal Commissions, departmental reviews and, more often than anyone admits, one scandal
    plus one newspaper campaign. The <strong>European</strong> layer shows sources are historical,
    not eternal: joining the EEC (1973, under the European Communities Act 1972) made
    directly applicable EU regulations supreme over conflicting UK law and channelled
    purposive interpretation into British courts. Brexit reversed the flow: the European
    Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018 converted the accumulated EU law body into UK
    "retained EU law" to avoid a cliff edge, and the Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform)
    Act 2023 recast it as "assimilated law" — tens of thousands of instruments whose status
    lawyers are still sorting out. Same words, new source: proof that the hierarchy is a
    human artefact with a changelog.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three refinements take you to expert level. First, the <strong>common law versus civil
    law</strong> contrast: England's system treats judicial precedent as a first-class source
    and procedural reasoning as case-driven, while continental civil-law systems (from the
    Napoleonic Code) treat codes as the source and precedent as guidance — though in practice
    both worlds interpret statutes and cite cases, differing in emphasis rather than kind.
    Second, the <strong>sovereignty puzzle</strong>: how can Parliament be supreme if the
    Human Rights Act, devolution statutes and assimilated EU law are "constitutional
    statutes" that courts interpret protectively? The orthodox answer — entrenchment by
    mutual political restraint, not by law — is the deepest open question in UK public law.
    Third, <strong>custom</strong>: the quiet bottom rung, from the law merchant of medieval
    merchants to international customary law (state practice plus <em>opinio juris</em>),
    which binds states without any legislature at all. The hierarchy, read carefully, is a
    map of who is trusted to make rules, and how much each kind of trust is worth.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Glanville Williams, Learning the Law; standard administrative law texts; the "What Makes a Law?" lesson covers precedent in depth; Law Commissions Act 1965; European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018.</p>
